Q: How to setup Spring JDBC Connection Pooling with Spring Boot? A: <p>Spring Boot 1.5.4 <strong>does not</strong> use HikariCP by default, so you must explicitly configure it.</p>
<p><em>Modify pom.xml to explicitly include HikariCP</em></p>
<pre><code>    &lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;com.zaxxer&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;HikariCP&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;2.6.3&lt;/version&gt;  &lt;!-- Compatible with Spring Boot 1.5.4 --&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>update your properties file</p>
<pre><code>    # Remote Database Configuration
spring.datasource.database2.url=jdbc:mysql://read-replica-database-production.cranmichpmc.us-west-2.rds.amazonaws.com/database2?zeroDateTimeBehavior=convertToNull
spring.datasource.database2.username=root
spring.datasource.database2.password=ret2a$$
spring.datasource.database2.driverClassName=com.mysql.jdbc.Driver

# Enable HikariCP
spring.datasource.database2.type=com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource

# HikariCP Connection Pool Settings
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.maximum-pool-size=10       # Adjust based on load
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.minimum-idle=2            # Maintain at least 2 idle connections
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.idle-timeout=30000        # Close idle connections after 30s
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.connection-timeout=60000  # Timeout if connection not available in 60s
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.max-lifetime=1800000      # Maximum lifetime of a connection (30 min)
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.leak-detection-threshold=2000  # Log connections not closed after 2s
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.auto-commit=true          # Auto-commit transactions
</code></pre>
<p>To avoid stale connections, configure HikariCP to validate connections before use:</p>
<pre><code>spring.datasource.database2.hikari.validation-query=SELECT 1
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.test-on-borrow=true
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.test-while-idle=true
spring.datasource.database2.hikari.test-on-return=true
</code></pre>
<p>Use HikariDataSource in Your Spring Configuration</p>
<p>Since you are managing multiple databases, create a custom HikariCP configuration:</p>
<pre><code>import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariConfig;
import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

@Configuration
public class DataSourceConfig {

    @Bean(name = &quot;database2DataSource&quot;)
    @ConfigurationProperties(prefix = &quot;spring.datasource.database2.hikari&quot;)
    public DataSource database2DataSource() {
        HikariConfig config = new HikariConfig();
        config.setJdbcUrl(&quot;jdbc:mysql://read-replica-database-production.cranmichpmc.us-west-2.rds.amazonaws.com/database2?zeroDateTimeBehavior=convertToNull&quot;);
        config.setUsername(&quot;root&quot;);
        config.setPassword(&quot;ret2a$$&quot;);
        config.setDriverClassName(&quot;com.mysql.jdbc.Driver&quot;);
        config.setMaximumPoolSize(10);
        config.setMinimumIdle(2);
        config.setIdleTimeout(30000);
        config.setConnectionTimeout(60000);
        config.setMaxLifetime(1800000);
        config.setLeakDetectionThreshold(2000);
        config.setAutoCommit(true);
        return new HikariDataSource(config);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>then restart ur server, your console have something like this</p>
<pre><code>HikariPool-1 - Starting...
HikariPool-1 - Added connection conn12345
HikariPool-1 - Pool stats (total=10, active=2, idle=8, waiting=0)
</code></pre>

